rlReadScreenPixels: the software renderer's pixels are top-down BGRA

With the software renderer (OPENGL_VERSION=Software), LoadImageFromScreen()
and TakeScreenshot() returned the frame upside down with red and blue
swapped. rlReadScreenPixels() treats glReadPixels() as OpenGL's (bottom row
first, RGBA) and flips the rows; rlsw's swReadPixels() instead hands out
the framebuffer the way it is presented -- rcore_desktop_sdl copies it to
the SDL window surface with the same call -- top row first and, with
RLSW_FRAMEBUFFER_OUTPUT_BGRA (the default), as BGRA. For the software
renderer, swap red and blue back and do not flip. Found by this tree's
raylib test, which draws a frame on SDL's dummy driver and reads it back;
not QNX-specific.

Upstream-Status: Pending

--- a/src/rlgl.h
+++ b/src/rlgl.h
@@ -3802,6 +3802,23 @@
     // WARNING: Getting alpha channel! Be careful, it can be transparent if not cleared properly!
     glReadPixels(0, 0, width, height, GL_RGBA, GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE, imgData);
 
+#if defined(GRAPHICS_API_OPENGL_SOFTWARE)
+    // rlsw hands out the framebuffer the way it is presented (rcore_desktop_sdl
+    // copies it to the window surface with the same call): top row first,
+    // and with red and blue swapped when RLSW_FRAMEBUFFER_OUTPUT_BGRA is set.
+    // Undo the swap; no vertical flip
+    for (int i = 0; i < width*height*4; i += 4)
+    {
+    #if RLSW_FRAMEBUFFER_OUTPUT_BGRA
+        unsigned char t = imgData[i];
+        imgData[i] = imgData[i + 2];
+        imgData[i + 2] = t;
+    #endif
+        imgData[i + 3] = 255;
+    }
+    return imgData;
+#endif
+
     // Flip image vertically
     // NOTE: Alpha value has already been applied to RGB in framebuffer, not needed anymore
     for (int y = height - 1; y >= height/2; y--)
